## Proposal 4 — compute scaling table (replace with measured numbers)

This is the biggest update. The v1 spec lists order-of-magnitude
estimates for an 8B model on 4×A100 80GB. The v1.1 example
(`examples/pythia_sweep.py`) and benchmark (`examples/_benchmark.py`)
ran on a single RTX 3090 Ti with `pythia-14m`. These numbers don't
replace the 8B/A100 estimates — they augment them with a *measured*
single-GPU reference point that future maintainers can compare
against.

**Current** (`CLAUDE.md` ### Compute scaling):

```
With `micro_batch_size=4` (activation checkpointing on, near memory ceiling),
per-rank backward ≈ 1.8 s, ~25k tok/s/GPU. Per `(ckpt, eval-batch)` cost for `B=32`:

| method | backwards | wallclock | notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `hutchinson` (n≈320, B-corrected) | ~320 | ~10 min | always applicable |
| `per_sequence_cv` (n≈32, +B=32) | ~64 | ~2 min | also gives cross-sample matrix |
| `opacus` (n≈10) | ~12 | ~22 s | not applicable to Pythia (tied embeddings) |

For a 50-checkpoint × 3-eval-batch sweep (150 pairs):
...
```

**Proposed**: keep the existing 8B / A100 / 4-GPU table as the
"reference target" estimate (it's still the right order of magnitude
for production sweeps), but **add** a measured single-GPU table
underneath, sourced from `examples/BENCHMARK.md`. Intentionally do not
delete the 8B/A100 numbers; future contributors will want both
reference points.

```
### Compute scaling — measured (pythia-14m, single RTX 3090 Ti)

Reference point from the v1.1 deployment example (see
`examples/BENCHMARK.md` for the full 18-row table). All numbers use
fp32 forward, S=64 for the benchmark, S=128 for the deployment
example, single GPU, no DDP. Same fixed seed.

| method            | n_h | B  | wall_s | peak_mb | notes                                |
|-------------------|----:|---:|------:|--------:|---------------------------------------|
| hutchinson        |   8 |  4 |  0.07 |     567 | toy upper bound for variance-vs-cost  |
| hutchinson        |  32 |  4 |  0.24 |     567 | sensible default at small B           |
| hutchinson        | 128 |  4 |  0.96 |     567 | n_h plateau on this fixture           |
| per_sequence_cv   |   8 |  4 |  0.08 |    1194 | matches hutchinson at this B          |
| per_sequence_cv   |  32 |  4 |  0.22 |    1194 | matches hutchinson at this B          |
| per_sequence_cv   | 128 |  4 |  0.88 |    1194 | matches hutchinson at this B          |
| hutchinson        |  32 |  8 |  0.32 |     885 | the example's chosen config           |
| per_sequence_cv   |  32 |  8 |  0.35 |    1875 | the example's auto-pick (B<=32)       |
| hutchinson        |  32 | 16 |  0.38 |    1529 |                                       |
| per_sequence_cv   |  32 | 16 |  0.64 |    3280 | per-sample grad cache visible here    |

For a 9-revision × 2-eval-batch × 1-method sweep at the example's
chosen config (`per_sequence_cv`, n=32, B=8, S=128) the measured
end-to-end wallclock — including model loading from a warm HF cache —
was **~13 s**, well inside the 15-minute budget the example targets.
The dominant cost on this hardware is checkpoint loading (~3 s per
revision after the first, which warms the cache); the chi_net
computation per call is ~1 s.

**Reading guidance:** the 8B/A100 estimates further down are
deliberately preserved as the production reference point. Use the
3090 Ti table only as a smoke check: if your toy or 1B-class model
runs at ~10× the wallclock of the rows above, your micro-batch is
probably wrong.
```

**Rationale**: replaces the order-of-magnitude guesses with a real
measurement on real published weights. The 8B/A100 reference is kept
because the production target is still 8B-class.

